References: <AANLkTikyoYJ2VWFtRT_KOoPUv2eHdtB9Tj1ineF2Xz4H@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <AANLkTinb2o7TPXm9BRbhGg4vPB7LxecJKUT28SoU4xgf@mail.gmail.com>

Hi, Vincent.

The zelig function is entirely controlled by the classes of the input data
and the model statement.  The multiply imputed data from Amelia *must *be
input using the following syntax:

zelig(fm, data = mi(a.out$imputations[[1]], a.out$imputations[[2]],
a.out$imputations[[3]], a.out$imputations[[4]], a.out$imputations[[5]]),
model="twosls")

because the mi() function in the data call will set the class of the list of
data frames that is input to zelig.

If you use the syntax above in place of the last line of your code, you will
get different estimates (one set of estimates for each imputed data frame).


The summary function does not work with a multi-level MI output from zelig,
however.  You'll have to summarize each model separately, or do the Rubin
rules by hand.

Best,
Olivia

